# Regression test: terminate on a job wrapping an external command (started via
# `cmd`) killed the real OS process fine, but the job's own bookkeeping stuck around in
# jobs forever afterward. Not specific to termination either -- a background job
# that simply finished on its own, never terminated and never even `fg`'d, leaked in
# exactly the same way when run non-interactively (as any .crush script is).
#
# Root cause: `ThreadStore::spawn` (src/lang/threads.rs) records a `ThreadData` entry
# per spawned thread that embeds a `CommandHandle`, and so a `JobHandle` clone -- kept
# alive in that registry until something explicitly calls `reap()`/`join_one()` for it,
# never automatically just because the underlying OS thread already returned. The
# interactive REPL calls `threads().reap(...)` after every line specifically to clean
# this up, but running a script through `execute::file`/`execute::string` never did, so
# that lingering clone kept `remove_finished_jobs`' `strong_count()` check
# (global_state.rs) from ever seeing the job as dead, no matter how long it waited or how
# many other jobs got created afterward.
#
# Fixed by calling `threads().reap(...)` from `create_job_handle_with_parent` itself --
# the same place `remove_finished_jobs` already runs on every new job's creation -- so
# reaping now happens lazily and uniformly regardless of whether crush is being driven
# interactively or by a script.
$job_id := $(cmd sleep 8171 &)
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=300)
terminate $job_id
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=500)

# `terminate` leaves a one-time snapshot behind (`JobData::terminated_snapshot`, see
# `src/lang/state/global_state.rs`) specifically so the *next* `jobs` listing can report
# "Terminated" for this job at least once -- see `tests/vt_terminate_and_signal_
# external_job.crush` -- rather than it silently vanishing before anyone ever gets a
# chance to see it died (an external `cmd` job's underlying thread can finish, and so
# get reaped, within microseconds of being killed -- long before any human or script
# could plausibly react). Pin that contract directly rather than just consuming past
# it: the first `jobs` call after termination must show exactly this one row, with
# status "Terminated"; only the *second* call (which consumes the snapshot) must show
# it gone.
#
# Matched by a distinctive sleep duration rather than by id: ids get recycled (see
# tests/job_orphaning_on_parent_death.crush), so a later job created here (a `jobs`
# pipeline itself) could plausibly be handed $job_id's own freed id, and filtering on
# `id == $job_id` would then wrongly match jobs' own still-running row instead of
# correctly finding nothing.
#
# `count`/field access are read from a plain variable assigned once up front
# (`$first_rows`/`$first_count`) before being asserted on, matching this file's own
# existing style for the second check below -- `| count` on an already-materialized
# table is otherwise fine to call more than once (confirmed directly: it reliably
# returns the same answer every time).
$first_rows := $(jobs | where {$definition:starts_with "\$cmd \"sleep\" 8171"} | materialize)
$first_count := $($first_rows | count)
assert ($first_count == 1) "a terminated external-command job should be reported once, with status Terminated, on the next jobs listing"
$first_status := ($first_rows[0]):status
assert ($first_status == "Terminated") "the one-time report of a terminated external-command job should have status Terminated"

$remaining_rows := $(jobs | where {$definition:starts_with "\$cmd \"sleep\" 8171"} | materialize)
$remaining_count := $($remaining_rows | count)
assert ($remaining_count == 0) "a terminated external-command job should eventually be reaped from jobs, not linger forever"
echo "done"
